{"name":"Gold Deposit","hex":"#e0ce57","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"deposit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Sediment or rock that is not native to its present location or is different from the surrounding material. Sometimes refers to ore or gems.","Bailment of personal property to be kept gratuitously for the bailor (depositor) and without any benefit to the bailee (depositary), e.g. for storage, carriage, repair, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To lay down; to place; to put.","To lay up or away for safekeeping; to put up; to store."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deposit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold mining","blocks":[{"text":"Gold mining involves the extraction of gold either via conventional mining or via placer mining, as it is a soft metal, gold is relatively easy to separate from its surrounding. Global gold production is estimated at approximately 4,000–5,000 metric tons per year, with the majority coming from newly mined sources. Historically, gold mining from alluvial deposits used manual separation processes, such as gold panning. The expansion of gold mining to ores that are below the surface has led to more complex extraction processes such as large scale pit mining and chemical extraction processes such as gold cyanidation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Super_Pit_Gold_Mine%2C_Kalgoorlie%2C_WA%2C_2023%2C_11.jpg/330px-Super_Pit_Gold_Mine%2C_Kalgoorlie%2C_WA%2C_2023%2C_11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_mining","wikidata":"Q1071389"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Alexander_Watt_-_The_History_of_a_Lump_of_Gold_%281885%29.pdf/page1-500px-Alexander_Watt_-_The_History_of_a_Lump_of_Gold_%281885%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Alexander_Watt_-_The_History_of_a_Lump_of_Gold_%281885%29.pdf/page1-500px-Alexander_Watt_-_The_History_of_a_Lump_of_Gold_%281885%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"History of a Lump of Gold, 1885","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlexander_Watt_-_The_History_of_a_Lump_of_Gold_(1885).pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Auriferous_brecciated_quartz-adularia_rhyolite_%28Sleeper_Rhyolite%2C_Miocene%3B_Sleeper_Mine%2C_Humboldt_County%2C_Nevada%2C_USA%29_1_%2814719259472%29.jpg/500p